-- title:
--   Characteristic-ℓ chart ring of X₀(p) over the j-line
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ and $\ell$ be primes with $\ell \neq p$, and let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $\ell$. Write $\bar\jmath =$ `jqModC K` for the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K$ of the integral power series $\mathrm{jNum} = E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$, i.e. the reduced $q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant, and let $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldFullC K p` be the intermediate field of $K \subseteq K((q))$ generated by the series $\bar\jmath(q^d)$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $p$. Let $B_0 =$ `CurveModel.chartRing K {\bar\jmath}` be the subalgebra of $F$ consisting of the elements integral over $K[\bar\jmath] = \mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\,K\,\{\bar\jmath\}$, regarded as a $K[X]$-algebra by evaluating polynomials at $\bar\jmath \in B_0$. The assertion is the conjunction of four statements: $B_0$ is a Dedekind domain; $B_0$ is a finite $K[X]$-module; $B_0$ has no zero $K[X]$-smul divisors; and, under the additional assumption that the $K[X]$-action on $B_0$ is faithful, the extension $\mathrm{Frac}(B_0)/\mathrm{Frac}(K[X])$ is separable. The last clause is thus conditional on faithfulness rather than unconditional.
--
--   The statement packages the standard facts that the integral closure of the $j$-line in the full level-$p$ modular function field in characteristic $\ell \neq p$ is a Dedekind domain, finite and torsion-free over $K[X]$, with separable function-field extension. In this form it supplies exactly the hypotheses needed to apply the general Dedekind-domain results on avoiding the ramification locus and on étaleness of quotients by level sets, and it is used in the construction of the étale quotients $B_0/(\bar g(\bar\jmath))$ attached to the characteristic-$\ell$ fibres of the level rings of $X_0(p)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_isDedekindDomain_and_finite_and_isSeparable_chartRing_jqModC.lean

